# Webots tricopter, matching worlds/webots_tricopter.wbt
#
# The world's rotors are calibrated for a thrust-to-weight of about 2.2, so
# hover sits near 45% throttle, and ardupilot_SITL_TRICOPTER.c linearises the
# propeller curve.  Yaw comes from the tilting tail rotor; the world keeps the
# propellers' own reaction torque negligible.
#
# The airframe's Robot node had no boundingObject/inertiaMatrix of its own, so
# Webots gave it the identity inertia tensor (1 kg m^2 per axis) on top of
# whatever the child Solids contributed -- tens to hundreds of times too high,
# and its two front rotor arms had no Solid/Physics of their own at all, so
# they contributed no mass or inertia either.  Both are now fixed in the world
# (a boundingObject on the Robot, and a nacelle Solid per arm, sized like the
# quad worlds' arms). ArduPilot's stock roll/pitch ATC gains fly the corrected
# airframe well.
#
# Yaw is a different story: it drives the tail rotor through a real,
# damped-but-not-instant HingeJoint servo, unlike a quad's near-instantaneous
# reaction-torque yaw.  That extra phase lag makes the stock ATC_RAT_YAW_P
# (0.3 in autotest's copter.parm) resonate -- a slowly-growing ~1 Hz yaw
# oscillation that roll/pitch stay clean through.  Lower P plus a little D
# damps it out; this is the standard fix for tricopter yaw wobble on real
# airframes too.  Retune together with the servo's controlPID/maxVelocity/
# maxTorque in the world file if either changes.
ATC_RAT_YAW_P    0.1
ATC_RAT_YAW_D    0.01

FRAME_CLASS      7
FRAME_TYPE       0

# MUST stay 0: the controller already linearises the propeller.
MOT_THST_EXPO    0

# true rotor speeds from Webots, via SIM_Webots.cpp -> AP_RPM SITL backend
RPM1_TYPE        10

ARMING_SKIPCHK   32

SIM_WIND_DIR     90.0
SIM_WIND_SPD     0.0
SIM_WIND_TURB    0.0
